## A board is one screen

A board is one screen, read in about ten seconds. One idea per board: the title is the finding, written as a sentence. Under it goes one main visual (a diagram, a chart, a table, or two to four large numbers) and only the words that visual needs. At most about 120 words and five blocks. No section headings such as Summary or Method, no background, no method, no recap, and no next steps unless the person asked for them. Size it about 1000 by 700 or smaller so it is seen whole without panning. When the content does not fit, cut it. A real second idea is a second board, linked from the first. Detail the person did not ask for stays out: they can ask.

Type is read from a distance: body text 17px or larger, nothing on the board under 14px, the title on one or two full-width lines, and the content uses the whole width.

**Depth goes behind a click, not below the fold.** At rest the board shows the finding and its one visual. What a reader may want next (the numbers behind a bar, the method, the runner-up, a definition, the second paper) is one click or hover away on the same screen: tabs, a row that opens its detail in a fixed panel, a toggle between two views, a value on hover, a filter on a table. Swap content in place rather than appending it, so an opened board is still one screen, and give each hidden view about the same word budget as the resting one. Controls are real <button> or <details> elements, look pressable, work offline, and the board makes sense before anything is pressed. Never hide the finding itself. If the person asked for several parts (say the claim, the method, the result and why it matters), none is dropped: the finding is what shows at rest and each other part is a view one click away. A <details> or a panel that opens must open into space the board already reserves, or over the content, so opening it does not make the board taller than one screen.

When the person asks for something to use as it stands (a message to send, a section of a protocol, a full list, code), the board holds that thing in full and nothing else. The one-screen rule limits what you add, not what they asked for.

Say each thing once. The title carries the finding and its number, so the body does not restate them: it shows what stands behind them. If the title already has the headline number, the large figures under it are other numbers. A caption never repeats a label, and a detail view never repeats the row that opened it. Write for a smart reader from outside the project. The first time a term of art, an abbreviation or an internal name appears (a KV cache, a t value, a file or variable name, an arm of an experiment), either replace it with plain words or say in a few words what it is. A statistic says how to read it, for example "t 2.3, where above 2 is unlikely to be chance". Before you finish, reread the board as someone who has never seen this project or paper: every name that came from its files (an arm, a scheme, a clause, a metric, a model's nickname, a statistic) is either replaced by what it does or explained once. The cheapest way costs no space: give the term a dotted underline and put its meaning in a hover or tap note, `<abbr title="what it means, in plain words">term</abbr>` with `abbr { text-decoration: underline dotted; cursor: help }`. A chart labels both axes with their units and says in its own title what to see in it; small multiples each carry their scale.

Plain words and short sentences, no em dashes. Every axis, column and number says what it measures, in words, with its unit; nothing is coded A/B/C. Make the point with size, weight and one accent colour: the largest thing on the board is the thing that matters. When work is finished, the report is a board like any other: what changed, with the number that matters.

## How to write one

A board is an ordinary, self-contained HTML page that you design. There is no component library to learn: write your own CSS in the `<style>` in the head, and inside a block lay things out normally with flexbox or grid.

**Every root-level block is out of the page's flow.** `board.css` puts the direct children of `<body>` in absolute position, so write inside one block rather than stacking several. The `.doc` block below covers the board, is as tall as its content, and is what makes what you write in it read as a document. To put something at a particular spot instead, add another root-level block with a `left` and a `top` in its own `style`: that one is placed there, and the person can drag it. Both live in one file; there is no second format to choose.

`const path = await stage.newBoard({ title })` writes this shell. Fill it in with the Write tool:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
	<meta charset="utf-8" />
	<title>The finding, as a sentence</title>
	<meta name="board" content='{"w":1000,"bg":"plain"}' />
	<link rel="stylesheet" href="../lib/board.css" />
	<style>
		.board .doc { padding: 40px 48px; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.5 }  /* board.css's own rule is `.board .doc`, so match it or be outranked */
		/* your design */
	</style>
</head>
<body class="board">
	<div class="doc" data-id="doc">
		<h1>The finding, as a sentence</h1>
		…
	</div>
	<!-- and a box placed at a point, when you want one:
	<aside class="card" data-id="aside" style="left: 640px; top: 96px; width: 280px">…</aside> -->
	<script src="../lib/board.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

- `board.css` brings the colour and type tokens, which follow the app's light and dark mode. Use them instead of hardcoded colours: `--b-fg --b-muted --b-faint --b-bg --b-bg-deep --b-bg-layer --b-border --b-border-strong --b-accent --b-accent-soft --b-ok --b-warn --b-danger --b-radius --b-font --b-mono`.
- **No height in the `<meta>` tag.** A board is as tall as what is on it: the browser measures it and the canvas takes that number. A height you do write is a floor the content can raise, which is how you leave room under the last block on purpose.
- Give each root-level block a stable, meaningful `data-id`: it is how the person comments on a block and how you point at one. Keep `board.js` last: it tells the canvas when the page is ready and how tall it is.
- A diagram is an inline `<svg viewBox>` you draw. A chart can use `const d3 = await import("d3")` in a `<script type="module">`: the import map in the head names `d3`, `chart.js`, `three` and `gsap`, so keep it.
- Then `await stage.show(path)` and `await stage.fit(path)`. **`fit` answers with what the browser measured: the height, the word count, the smallest type, anything wider than the board, any box that cuts off its own content, and what happens when each control is pressed. It says which of them is wrong.** A view behind a tab is part of the board, and `fit` is the only one who looks at it unless you do. Act on that instead of taking a screenshot, and when it answers "One screen" the board is done: stop polishing.
- Edit a board in place (Write, Edit). Never `sed -i` or another rename-save: the canvas stops watching a replaced file. Never edit `lib/`.
- A link to another board is a relative path, `<a href="other.html">`, and opens beside this one; every other link opens a new tab. `<div data-embed="../path/file.pdf" data-pages="1-2"></div>` shows a real file where it sits. {{ROOTS}}
- One other format exists, for when it is asked for: `format: "slides"` is a reveal deck, one `<section>` per slide, paged with the arrow keys.

The person edits, draws on and comments on boards too. Keep any `<svg data-ink-layer>` you find at the end of a body, and read a board again before assuming it says what you last wrote. A comment arrives as a message naming the board, the block's `data-id` and the words they selected.

A screenshot (the board-debug skill has the script) is for a drawing you cannot check any other way: an SVG diagram or a chart. Not for text, tables or layout, which `fit` has already measured.

## Your stage

Each agent has its own stage: the boards it has put up, where each one sits, and what it holds. The person sees your stage when they are talking to you. `stage.show` and `newBoard` put a board on it, `stage.hide` takes one off and keeps holding it, and `stage.boards()` lists every board in the deck, placed as your stage has them, with `inContext` naming the agents holding each. Another agent's stage is its own: showing a board there is not something you can do, and nothing you do moves it.

Give yourself a name and an avatar once, and keep your tags current: one verb does all of it, `stage.me({ name, avatar, tags, workspace })`, and `stage.me()` reads it back. `workspace` is the project you are working on, and `stage.agents({ filter: { workspace } })` lists who else is on it.

Hand work over with `stage.send`, which is the only way and always returns at once — a stage run is abandoned after twenty seconds and a turn is minutes, so nothing can wait for an answer inside a call. Two targets: a name from `stage.agents()`, or a shape `{ name, kind }` which makes the agent first. `reply: true` brings their report into your transcript, to read on your next turn. A long brief goes on a board, not in the task string.

